Stage 02: elliptic (Winslow) smoothing tested - does NOT fix the tip fold (2026-08-13)
- Implemented winslow_smooth_2d in stage02_common.py: proper elliptic smoothing solving
  alpha*x_xixi - 2*beta*x_xieta + gamma*x_etaeta = 0, i.e. computational coordinates
  harmonic in physical space. This is the maximum-principle smoother RUNBOOK S3 names,
  and is fundamentally different from the Laplacian averaging used earlier.
- RESULT: interior-only smoothing made it slightly WORSE (-0.0363 -> -0.0499, converged
  by 200 iterations). Freeing the block interface so it could relax made it far worse
  (-0.9958), because each patch extrapolated its interface independently and the blocks
  then disagreed there.
- WHY IT CANNOT HELP, now pinpointed: the folds in tip_lower span j-layers 0 through 7
  of 8 - nearly the entire radial extent, 56 cells - not a boundary-adjacent corner
  artefact. Winslow only moves patch interiors, so it cannot repair a patch whose inner
  and outer boundaries are themselves badly related.
- REVISED DIAGNOSIS, the most precise yet: the inner ring locally leaves the section.
  For this cambered/reflexed lower surface a uniform shrink toward the centroid does not
  stay inside the contour, so the inner and outer rings cross and the patch inverts over
  a wide region rather than at a corner. Every inner-ring construction tried so far
  (centroid shrink, camber-directed offset, inward-bisector offset, Laplacian smoothing,
  arc-length re-parameterisation) shares that same weakness.
- WHAT WOULD ACTUALLY ADDRESS IT: an inner ring guaranteed to lie inside the section -
  medial-axis or inward-offset-with-collision-detection - or a genuine MULTIBLOCK
  elliptic solve over the whole cap with only the OUTER ring fixed and interface nodes
  shared and updated once, rather than per-patch. Neither is a parameter change; both
  are new construction work.
